  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00007` — **Sync I/O in Hot Path**, `src/api/files.ts:12–15`, confidence 0.9.\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n```\n\"`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)\",\n\"`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)\",\n\"`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)\",\n\"lines: 12, 13, 15\",\n\"swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it\"\n```\n\nSummary, verbatim: *\"3 synchronous I/O calls inside domain function — each invocation blocks the event loop for the duration of the I/O. Async counterparts exist for every method captured.\"*\n\n## The specific sync methods\n\nThree, all imported from `node:fs` at line 6 and all inside `loadPlanSchema`:\n\n1. `existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` — L12\n2. `statSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` — L13\n3. `readFileSync(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\")` — L15\n\nThat's **three blocking filesystem round-trips per invocation**, on the same path. Under concurrency they serialize: the event loop cannot service any other request while any one of them is in the kernel. That is exactly the p99-plus-loop-lag signature described — the mean stays fine because each syscall is fast in isolation; the tail explodes because every concurrent request queues behind every other request's three syscalls.\n\nThe JSON is a further, smaller blocker — `JSON.parse` on L15 is also synchronous — but it is not the fix here. Parse cost is CPU-bound and proportional to schema size; the syscalls are the per-request stall the detector caught.\n\n## The smallest async migration\n\n```ts\nimport { readFile, stat } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n\nexport async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {\n  try {\n    const info = await stat(SCHEMA_PATH);\n    if (info.size === 0) return null;\n    return JSON.parse(await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\"));\n  } catch {\n    return null;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWhat this preserves: the name, the zero-parameter arity, and the return contract — `null` for missing-or-empty, parsed JSON otherwise. The `existsSync` guard is absorbed by the `catch`, because `stat` on a missing path rejects with `ENOENT` and the old code returned `null` in that case anyway.\n\nOne honest caveat on \"preserves the public signature\": the return type necessarily becomes `Promise<unknown>` instead of `unknown`, and callers must `await`. There is no way around this — a function that blocks the event loop cannot stop blocking it while still returning synchronously. That's the irreducible cost of the fix, and it's the whole reason this bug survives code review: the migration is viral at the call site. Here it is cheap to pay: `grep` finds **no in-repo callers** of `loadPlanSchema`, so nothing internal needs updating.\n\nIf you want it smaller still, collapse to a single syscall — `readFile` alone, treating empty content as the empty case:\n\n```ts\nexport async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {\n  try {\n    const text = await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\");\n    return text.length === 0 ? null : JSON.parse(text);\n  } catch {\n    return null;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSame observable contract, one filesystem round-trip per call instead of three. I'd ship this version: it fixes the blocking *and* removes two of the three I/O operations, without introducing any cache.\n\n## What I deliberately did not do\n\n- **No caching.** Memoizing the schema would flatten the latency graph while leaving three blocking syscalls in the code path for the first request and for every cache miss/invalidation. The event loop stall would return the moment the cache was cleared or the process restarted under load. You asked for the blocking call fixed, and it is.\n- **No edits applied.** `src/api/files.ts` is a scanner fixture — its header comment says the sync I/O is intentional, and it's the input that produces this finding. Fixing it in place would break the eval baseline. Apply the patch to your real handler, not here.\n\n## One note on the ranking\n\nThis finding is `severity: \"low\"` and sits 7th in the list, below several naming and metadata findings — which is a bad look for the thing that's actually paging you. The reason is visible in its own scores: `churn: 0`, `blast_radius: 0`, `test_gap: 0.5`, `recency: 0`. The fixture has no git history, so the two signals that would have lifted a live production hot path — how often this file changes and how many modules import it — are both floored at zero. In a real repo with history, a handler like this would rank considerably higher. Worth knowing before you use the severity column to triage.\n\nAlso on this file, unrelated to the latency but in the blast zone of any edit you make here: `crime_00018` flags `SCHEMA_PATH` as `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` — a path that exists on exactly one machine. Your async `readFile` will reject with `ENOENT` on every other host and return `null` silently, which will look like \"the schema is missing\" rather than \"the path is wrong.\"",
